 **Public words give no resolved gain in pricing markets.** For gpt-6-luna sellers, adding public messages to the auditor's price view changed AUROC by −0.01 [−0.09, +0.06] on the locked test, so gains above +0.06 are excluded. Text alone scored 0.47 there, and for both Qwen3.8 arms it was *inverted* (0.21 and 0.15): the auditor ranked channel games below controls. In the Qwen3.8 base arm, channel sellers invited rivals publicly less often than controls (0.40 vs 0.88; post hoc), which fits the inversion but was not shown to cause it. Text does carry signal on a different task. On external SALT-NLP peer-verification data, where agents later submit a verbal verdict, the same kind of auditor predicted later rule-breaking verdicts from preceding messages with AUROC 0.87, even though, in the full dataset, 62% of rule-breaking episodes contain no explicit agreement. This is a different construct, so it does not explain why text failed in the pricing markets.

-**A channel is not collusion.** A private notepad with no recipient raised the pair premium by +1.04, against +1.61 for a real channel: a ratio of 0.65 [0.34, 1.32], about two-thirds. With memory and long-run profit framing, 19 of 20 no-channel gpt-6-luna markets priced at least 2 above Nash (rounds 11–40), and the channel added no detectable premium. Abliterated models showed no detectable price increase over their base versions in 3/3 Qwen pairs. Only 14 of 50 communication-positive channel runs followed through on their agreement under our execution rule. Level screens also flag legitimate advantages: 7/10 high-cost and 9/10 quality-edge sellers vs 2/10 controls. Finally, in the prices-only market (gpt-6-luna) the channel effect was significant in only two of five prompt wordings.
